#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A fake `tg` for live runs of the dialogs pane (docs/design/dialogs-pane-ux.md §8 T0a).

Put this folder first on PATH, under the name `tg`:

    mkdir -p "$LAB/bin" && ln -sf "$PWD/mods/dialogs-pane/dev/fake-tg" "$LAB/bin/tg"
    PATH="$LAB/bin:$PATH" FAKE_TG_DIR="$LAB/state" claude --plugin-dir mods/dialogs-pane ...

Placeholder chats and folders only (repo rule): nothing leaves the machine. It
answers the calls the pane makes, with tg's JSON shapes (README "Output"):
dialogs, read, msg, whoami, allow list, send (--dry-run too), press, delete,
watch, folders [--counts | --chats], dialogs --folder. Its guard honours
--yes as tg's does: a write to a chat outside ALLOW needs --yes (exit 7). A
write that reaches BotFather exits 1 and is logged: the pane must refuse it
before tg runs, as tg itself has no such rule.

State lives in $FAKE_TG_DIR (default: $TMPDIR/fake-tg): calls.log has one line
per call, and `tg watch` prints the lines appended to watch.jsonl, so a live
event is `echo '{"event": "message", ...}' >> "$FAKE_TG_DIR/watch.jsonl"`.

Folders (T9): fifteen placeholder folders, All chats third, so the strip
overflows at 64 and 100 columns; Work holds 60 chats (Contact 10 to Contact 69)
for the ring checks. `echo 40 > "$FAKE_TG_DIR/flood"` makes the next
`folders --chats` exit 5 with FLOOD_WAIT 40s (the file is removed), as tg does
past flood_sleep_threshold. The pass bumps a chat that `watch.jsonl` wrote to,
so a later pass agrees with the live events.
"""

